      "practice_mapping_verdict": "PASS",
      "summary": "Verified against current eCFR (up to date as of 2026-09-15), uscode.house.gov, malegislature.gov and mass.gov. Every federal figure is correctly attributed: the 3.6 g limit to 21 CFR 1314.20(a) and 21 U.S.C. 830(d)(1) (the statute says daily amount, the regulation single calendar day); the mobile-vendor 7.5 g/30-day limit to 1314.20(b) and 830(e)(1)(A)(ix)(II); the mail-order limits to 1314.100 and 830(e)(2)(B) for persons reporting under 1310.03(c); and the 9 g/30-day figure, with its 7.5 g shipping sub-limit, to the purchaser prohibition in 21 U.S.C. 844(a), which is absent from 1314.20. Packaging (1314.05), placement and delivery (1314.25), the logbook, ID, signature methods, warning notice, two-year retention, central-location notice and State-record substitution (1314.30), disclosure, use limits and immunity (1314.45), training and per-location self-certification with the 12-23 month group and annual update (1314.35, 1314.40), the $21 fee and pharmacy waiver at 1314.42(b), loss reporting with the 15-day written report and supplier in-transit duty (1314.15) and 1314.50 all match the text. The single-package 60 mg exception is correctly extended to identification by 830(e)(1)(A)(iv), which applies ID and signature only to sales requiring a logbook entry, although 1314.30(b) read alone is unqualified; the section states the statutory basis. 842(a)(14) reaches the seller and its employees and agents, 842(c)(1)(A) supplies the civil penalty and 842(c)(2) the knowing-violation crime, as stated. The Massachusetts no-added-requirement statement is framed as a dated result over named sources; a scan of all sections of c. 94C, every listed 247 CMR chapter and the current 105 CMR 700.000 found no provision on these products. The s. 4 conditions and s. 5 terms are quoted accurately; 105 CMR 700.002 adopts 21 CFR 1308.11-1308.15 and defines Schedule VI without any exception, and no s. 4 exception appears in 105 CMR 720, 721, 722, 724 or 726 or in 247 CMR; s. 17(a) and (c) require an electronic prescription for Schedules II-VI, so 1306.26(f) is unmet; 811(g)(1) is limited to non-narcotic drugs and 811(g)(3) to the three stated categories, so an opium preparation (802(17)) could not be federally excepted. 1306.26(a)-(g) and the 1300.01 and c. 94C, s. 1 pharmacist definitions are accurate. Section 27 is now one sentence naming sellers; St. 2006, c. 172 had added the age-18 limit and proof-of-age ID sentence, both struck by St. 2018, c. 208, ss. 47-48, with s. 49 striking the age words from s. 32I(d); c. 111, s. 25K's passive insert sentence is described accurately, and the Board advisory's recommendations are labelled as guidance throughout. Minor phrasing simplifications considered and not treated as defects: quick_review[4] says 'shipped' for 'imported by means of shipping' (exceptions[8] and the table use the exact words); decision_logic[10] and worked_examples[4] state the Board-recommended no-ID, no-cap, no-record course as the action while attributing it to the advisory. Practice questions MA-Q-0107 (single 60 mg package exception), MA-Q-0108 (separate certification per place of business) and MA-Q-0114 (route syringe sale through a s. 27 seller such as the pharmacist) are each answerable from, and not contradicted by, the section. No defect found; publishable as it stands."
